 {:seon.task/name "the-adapter-runtimes-buffers-are-freed-at-the-real-exit"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the audit of host/chez's dynamic-winds (Jolt jolt/a-read-scope-holds-across-a-park dfdc33ef: every winder whose cleanup closes or frees what its body uses takes jolt-finally-in, which a park or preemption drops, so the cleanup runs at the real exit; a plain winder runs at the park, and the resumed body meets a closed port or freed memory, as a fork server's zipdir-read did at boot). Two are left, in host/chez/scheme-adapter-runtime.ss: the winder of sa-uname-field (foreign-free buf; its before-thunk zeroes the buffer, so it also runs again on every rewind) and the one in sa-windows-env's windows-block (the Windows environment block, free block); both still plain dynamic-winds. That file loads before values.ss defines jolt-finally-in (fibers.ss captures the marker by guarded reference for the same reason), so the marker cannot be named there as the others are: the setup moves out of the before-thunk, and the cleanup either takes the marker by the same guarded, late-bound reference or moves into a file loaded after values.ss. Proof: a preemption stepped through each body (the scheduler's quantum, as zip-index-preempt-test does for zipdir-read) answers the body's value, and the buffer is freed once at the end."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{jolt.async-test/a-preempted-adapter-call-frees-its-buffers}}
