 {:seon.task/name "the-main-carrier-test-ends-the-same-way-every-run"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-29: `make fibers` failed once in Jolt's test/chez/main-carrier-test.clj (the check \"a wait nothing can end is told so in its caller, which goes on\") with \"condition-wait: mutex N is recursively locked\" and \"nil where a java.lang.Number is required\"; the file passed 3 of 3 alone and the gate passed on rerun. A niced rerun of the file hung (0% CPU, 19 Chez sweeper threads in S_mutex_acquire, 3 fibers parked). Done (Jolt 80579b46): the main carrier's loop holds carrier 0's mutex with jolt-with-mutex, so a throw in its idle-wait region releases it; test/chez/main-carrier-throw-test.clj makes the next jolt-timer-before? raise there and checks the error surfaces as itself and jolt-locks-assert-none! holds. Still open, as that commit says: main-carrier-test.clj ended differently in some runs even with the region protected (20 niced runs on stock Chez: 17 OK, 2 hangs with the sweeper pool in S_mutex_acquire, 1 failure with the same two messages), so the second hold of carrier 0's mu is not a throw out of this region and its first cause is not found. Since then the loop's stuck decision changed (Jolt 0e4fb237, 869d77f6), and the file has not been re-measured. Next: 20 niced runs of the file, then read the first failure's cause (jolt-timer-before? or mono-ms-until reading a stale next; a handler that locks carrier 0's mu). A gate that fails one run in N is red until fixed (a flaky result is a defect: docs/health.md#a-flaky-test). Proof: 20 runs of the file ending the same way."
