 {:seon.task/name "a-value-a-remote-end-cannot-encode-answers-its-asker"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-09 diagnosing stage 5's :sync-up stall (verified on the sync worktree's root): root sent an agent a reply holding a sorted map (seon.writer/written's :unfiled) that konserve.jolt.fasl/encode cannot encode (no stable encoding for a value of this type). Root's send pump caught it and called dead! (jolt/async/remote.clj:441), closing root's end; agent-proc read nil from :from as :dead (seon.agent-proc, agent_proc.clj:286); end-agent parked the outer eval under ::dying to be answered at the process's exit (root.clj:382), and the live process never exited, so the eval waited forever; the exception was recorded nowhere. Exists on main today for any unencodable value root sends an agent. The class, three defects: (a) an encode failure on one side is reported as the peer's death; (b) a death is assumed without the process ending; (c) the error is swallowed. Correct: the remote pump answers a value it cannot encode with that value's typed error to the request it answered and keeps the end open (a Jolt fix on a jolt/<topic> branch, seantempesta/jolt); root routes the error to its asker; an end closed while its pid lives is recorded as a stall, never waited on as a death. Then sweep root's replies to agents for values jfasl cannot encode. Done on jolt/remote-encode (Jolt 88c36ac0, seon.agent-proc/unencodable, root's ::dying stall): the writer sends (encode-error v t) in the value's place and keeps the end open; an answer (:reply, :fx-reply) reaches its asker's :k with the typed :seon.agent/unencodable; a request of root's (its :id) is answered at its rid by agent-frame from an {:op :unsendable} frame the hook puts on the end's :in; an end closed on a live pid ends that agent at once (seon.agent-proc's kill) and its exit, which comes, answers the requests it held through ::dying, with no stall timer (seon.procs-test/an-end-closed-on-a-live-pid-ends-the-agent-and-answers-its-waiter), so the old item (3), a stall recorded only after a bound, is done. What remains: (1) a frame the READER cannot decode (jolt/async/remote.clj's reader, whose catch Throwable still calls dead! and rethrows, and whose header calls an undecodable value a defect) still closes both halves (jolt/async/remote.clj reader: the catch Throwable calls dead! and rethrows), the same class from the other side: the peer is alive and the end is lost, and root reads it as the agent's death. Reproduce: open a pair (test/chez/remote-chan-test.clj's way) whose receiving codec :decode throws on one marked value, send it then a second value: the second must arrive, and the sender should learn its value was refused (needs a back-channel: the reader answers on its own :out with a refusal frame the peer's writer pump delivers as the error value to the sender's :in, so the error reaches the asker as it does for encode); today the end is dead and the second never arrives. (2) a request frame of an agent's own that root cannot decode answers nobody (the agent waits on its :k): the same repro with the agent as sender. Evidence: the diagnosis in the Optimization session, 2026-11-09; reproduce with an agent that defines a function in a new namespace then calls (seon.agent/sync-up!) from an eval on jolt/sync before its narrow fix."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/a-reply-the-wire-cannot-encode-answers-its-asker-and-the-end-serves-on seon.procs-test/a-request-the-wire-cannot-encode-answers-roots-asker-and-nothing-waits seon.procs-test/an-end-closed-on-a-live-pid-ends-the-agent-and-answers-its-waiter}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-main-carrier-run-ends-when-its-thunk-does"
  :seon.task/plan "Filed 2026-11-10 (Deep fixes), from a hang of its own making: a forked test child ran (jolt.host/main-carrier-run! f) with four fibers that loop on jolt.host/fiber-yield; f threw (a contract error), and the process stayed at 99 percent CPU for 9 minutes until killed, with nothing on its stderr (evidence, outside the repository: the session's notes folder, main-fiber-dead-process-spins-2026-11-10/fiber-dump.txt: four fibers ready or running, none parked, the main fiber gone). Reproduction: (jolt.host/main-carrier-run! (fn [] (jolt.host/fiber-spawn (fn [] (loop [] (jolt.host/fiber-yield) (recur)))) (throw (ex-info \"x\" {})))) never returns; the same with the thunk returning a value. Cause, read in the Jolt fork (host/chez/fibers.ss, ``jolt-main-carrier-run/loop!``): the loop asks whether its thunk ended only after ``jolt-fiber-drain!`` returns, and the drain returns only when the run queue is empty, so while any fiber stays ready the end of the thunk, value or throw, is never seen. What should happen: main-carrier-run! answers its thunk's value, or raises its throw, as soon as the thunk's fiber ends, whatever else is ready; a process whose body is that thunk then ends with the error on its stderr and a non-zero exit, as seon.lib/fork! already does for a child that throws. Whose: main-carrier-run! is our fork's (upstream has none), so the fix is in the Jolt fork: the drain stops when the thunk's fiber is done (one test of the box between dispatches). Who runs on a main carrier here: root's boot (seon.root, up to its first thread) and every agent's whole body (seon.repl); a fork server and a test child inherit an agent's or root's. They are stranded only while some fiber stays ready for ever: a fiber parked on a socket or a channel is not ready, so today's procs do not strand them, but one go block that spins (an agent's own eval can make one) keeps a process whose body has ended alive and silent. Proof when built: the reproduction above answers its throw (and its value) within a bounded count of dispatches; red today (it never returns: run it in a forked child and wait on the child's exit)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{jolt.async-test/jolt-host-main-carrier-run-thunk-spawns-fiber}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-parked-fiber-nothing-can-wake-is-told"
  :seon.task/plan "Filed 2026-11-10 (Deep fixes, asked by Context; its task is ``seon.repl/an-eval-that-never-returns-gives-the-model-its-turn-back``). An agent's eval parked for ever on a put to a channel nothing else held (Context's hang of 2026-11-10: fiber 0 parked on an alt-handler inside run-interruptible, five flow procs alive in alts), and the agent's REPL was blocked for good. What exists (read in the Jolt fork, host/chez/fibers.ss): when the whole program is asleep (no fiber queued, no timer pending, no descriptor awaited, every other thread parked) each parked fiber is told once by a typed error thrown from its own wait; and the fiber registry is weak, so a fiber parked on a channel nobody else holds is collected. Neither covers this case: other fibers are parked on sockets, so the program can still wake, and the stuck fiber is held from elsewhere (it is the process's body), so it is not collected. The fact that decides it is reachability, which only the collector has: the fiber can be woken only through the channel it waits on, so it can never be woken when nothing live holds that channel but the waiter's own handler. Design, not built: a parked fiber's wait holds its channel weakly as well; after a collection, a channel held only weakly and by its own waiters gives each of those waits the typed error (the all-asleep error's sibling, naming the channel and the wait), thrown from the wait as any throw. No clock. Where: the Jolt fork, with the fiber slice of the upstream merge (the fiber and timer code is being re-fitted there). Not read: jolt.async's channel and handler records, and what holds a channel besides its waiters (a go block's closure, a mult, a timeout). Proof when built: a fiber that puts to a channel only it holds, beside a fiber parked on a socket, answers the typed error after a collection; a channel another live fiber holds is never told. Should cost: nothing on a park but one weak pointer; the check runs at a collection, over parked fibers."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{jolt.async-test/fiber-puts-channel-only-holds-beside-fiber-parked}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-remote-end-whose-writer-died-ends-its-peers-wait"
  :seon.task/plan "Filed 2026-11-10 (Deep fixes; measured on the Jolt merged with upstream, where upstream's removed call form (jolt.ffi/write-array p arr off n) made the writer throw). What exists: a jolt.async.remote end's writer pump that throws catches it, closes the end's own channels (dead!) and rethrows (stdlib/jolt/async/remote.clj writer); the descriptor closes only when both pumps have finished (finish!). Its reader is still waiting on the socket, so the socket stays open, the peer reads no end of stream, and the peer's take on :in waits for ever. On a thread pump the throw prints; on a fiber pump nothing printed. Reproduction: a socket pair (remote/socketpair), an end opened on each side with either pump, a writer made to throw (an encode whose error answer cannot be encoded either, or the removed write-array form): the put is accepted, the take on the other end's :in never answers (3 s bound in the reproduction, target/upstream-work/jolt/tmp/re2.clj of that session; nothing kept in the tree). Correct: an end that dies for a reason of its own shuts its socket down in both directions (the shutdown close! already makes for the read side), so its own reader ends and the peer reads end of stream: the peer's :in closes after what arrived, and a take answers nil. The throw is reported once, on either pump (the fiber pump's through the go/fiber body's report). Where: the Jolt fork, stdlib/jolt/async/remote.clj dead! and the writer's catch. Not read: whether a reader that throws has the same hole toward the peer's writer. Proof when built (test/chez/remote-chan-test.clj): a writer that throws closes the peer's :in, both pumps, and the process is not told a deadlock. Should cost: one shutdown(2) on a path that is already an error."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-fiber-dump-names-a-running-fiber"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: the fiber dump (SIGINFO, `jolt-fiber-dump`) prints frames only for a parked fiber; a running one printed `no saved continuation (running)` and a go block's step `the procedure ?`, so the one fiber one wants named in a spin (a root's stop, a fork server's warm-up at 22 G ticks, 2026-11-11) had no name. Built, on the fork's branch ``jolt/a-running-fiber-is-named`` (on `601c9379`, not in a pin): the dump lists, for a fiber it has no frames for, the code labels and vars its entry thunk (or its step) and the closures inside it hold, three closures deep; on a small program a spinning go block prints `entered through [jfn$fd.demo/go-spinner$0]`. Not shown: a real root's dump mid-spin; a spinning OS thread is no fiber and is not in the dump. Done means: it rides a Jolt pin with a gate (a program with a spinning fiber, the dump naming its function)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
